Arena allocation trades individual `free` for bulk release. That is exactly the right trade for a compiler pass or a request handler: many short-lived objects, one lifetime, and no pointer-tracking overhead per allocation.

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    unsigned char *base;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t offset;
} arena;

static size_t align_up(size_t value, size_t alignment) {
    /* alignment must be a power of two */
    return (value + alignment - 1) & ~(alignment - 1);
}

arena *arena_new(size_t capacity) {
    arena *a = malloc(sizeof *a);
    if (a == NULL) return NULL;
    a->base = malloc(capacity);
    if (a->base == NULL) { free(a); return NULL; }
    a->capacity = capacity;
    a->offset = 0;
    return a;
}

void *arena_alloc(arena *a, size_t size, size_t alignment) {
    size_t aligned = align_up(a->offset, alignment);
    if (aligned > a->capacity || size > a->capacity - aligned) {
        return NULL;   /* caller decides whether to grow or fail */
    }
    void *block = a->base + aligned;
    a->offset = aligned + size;
    /* Zeroing keeps callers from reading indeterminate padding. */
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size; i++) ((unsigned char *)block)[i] = 0;
    return block;
}

void arena_reset(arena *a) { a->offset = 0; }

void arena_free(arena *a) {
    if (a == NULL) return;
    free(a->base);
    free(a);
}
```

Three points worth stating explicitly. The overflow check is written as `size > a->capacity - aligned` rather than `aligned + size > a->capacity`, because the latter can wrap on a 32-bit target and accept a wildly oversized request - a classic heap-overflow vulnerability. `arena_reset` is the fast path that makes the arena worthwhile, and it is safe only because every allocation shares one lifetime. And the alignment argument must be a power of two for the mask trick; if callers can pass arbitrary values, validate with `(alignment & (alignment - 1)) == 0` and return `NULL` otherwise, since a non-power-of-two would silently produce a misaligned pointer that faults only on strict-alignment architectures.

Error boundaries must still be class components - there is no hook equivalent, because `componentDidCatch` is the only place React reports a render failure. Wrap it once and expose a hook-friendly API around it.

```tsx
import { Component, type ErrorInfo, type ReactNode } from "react";

type Props = { children: ReactNode; onError?: (error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) => void; fallback?: (retry: () => void) => ReactNode };
type State = { error: Error | null };

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): State {
    return { error };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) {
    // Report once, here. Do not log in render: React may render twice in dev.
    this.props.onError?.(error, info);
  }

  retry = () => this.setState({ error: null });

  render() {
    const { error } = this.state;
    if (error) {
      return this.props.fallback ? this.props.fallback(this.retry) : <DefaultFallback error={error} onRetry={this.retry} />;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

Four behaviours worth knowing before shipping one. A boundary catches errors in its *children's* render, lifecycle, and constructors - not in event handlers, `setTimeout`, or async callbacks, which need ordinary `try/catch`. `getDerivedStateFromError` must be static and pure; it runs during the render phase and cannot perform side effects, which is why reporting lives in `componentDidCatch`. React re-mounts the whole subtree when the state flips back to `null`, so a retry that fails for a deterministic reason will simply error again - keep the retry button and surface the error rather than looping. And place boundaries lower than the app root: one boundary around the whole app turns a failure in a single widget into a blank page, whereas a boundary per panel degrades gracefully. Clearing the error state cannot fix a bug in the component's own props; if the same props always fail, the retry should remount with fresh data, which means a `key` change rather than a state reset.

Embedding promotes the fields and methods of one type into another, so callers can reach them directly. It is composition with syntactic sugar, not inheritance: there is no subtyping, no virtual dispatch, and the embedded type does not know about its container.

The direction of the delegation is what people get wrong. Methods on the outer type win; methods on the embedded type are reachable as if they were defined on the outer type, but when an embedded method calls another method, it calls its own, not the outer override.

```go
type Logger struct{}
func (Logger) Log(msg string) { fmt.Println(msg) }

type Service struct {
    Logger            // embedded: Service.Log exists
    name string
}

func (s Service) Start() { s.Log("starting " + s.name) }

// Explicit interface satisfaction comes free:
var _ interface{ Log(string) } = Service{}
```

The refactor that embedding makes pleasant is extracting shared behaviour from two types that both need it. Rather than a base class with a constructor calling a virtual method - the classic fragile-base-class problem - each type embeds the same helper and can override any promoted method by declaring its own. The compiler reports ambiguity rather than guessing when two embedded types each provide a method with the same name, so a diamond produces a compile error at the call site instead of a silent preference for the shallower type.

Two rules keep it readable. Embed when you want the inner type's whole API exposed, because that is what embedding means; if you want to expose only two methods, hold a named field and write the two forwarding methods, which makes the surface explicit and searchable. And avoid embedding a type from another package purely to reach its unexported behaviour - that couples you to internals the package author did not promise. For interfaces, embedding an interface in another interface is the idiomatic way to compose contracts without repeating method sets.

A schema is worth writing when it is the source of truth for validation rather than documentation that drifts, which is why the keyword choices below are the strict ones.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["customer_id", "amount_cents", "currency"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "customer_id": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^cus_[a-z0-9]{20}$"
    },
    "amount_cents": {
      "type": "integer",
      "minimum": 1,
      "maximum": 100000000
    },
    "currency": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": ["USD", "EUR", "GBP"]
    },
    "description": {
      "type": ["string", "null"],
      "maxLength": 500
    },
    "line_items": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 100,
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "required": ["sku", "quantity"],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "properties": {
          "sku": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1 },
          "quantity": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 1000 }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Why each keyword is there. `additionalProperties: false` turns a typo'd field into a 422 naming the unknown key instead of a silently ignored one - the single highest-value keyword in a request schema, and the one most often omitted because it is not the default. `maximum` on the integer matters because a `minimum`-only schema accepts a value that overflows the column or the payment provider's limit, and the failure then appears downstream. `type: ["string", "null"]` is the draft 2020-12 form for a nullable field; `nullable: true` is an OpenAPI 3.0 construct that a strict validator rejects. On the response side, leave `additionalProperties` unset so adding a field is not a breaking change for a strict consumer.

Two semantics worth stating because they surprise people. `required` in JSON Schema means the key must be *present*, not non-empty: `{"currency": ""}` satisfies a schema that requires `currency` unless `minLength` is set. And `pattern` is not anchored by default in some validators, so `"^cus_[a-z0-9]{20}$"` uses explicit anchors rather than relying on the implementation - the difference shows up as a value with trailing junk passing validation.

`@contextmanager` converts a generator function into a context manager object. When you write `with cm():` Python calls `cm()`, gets the generator, calls `next()` on it once, and runs everything up to the `yield`. The `with` body then executes against whatever was yielded. On exit, Python calls `next()` again: execution resumes at the `yield`, and your cleanup code runs. The generator then raises `StopIteration`, which the decorator catches and treats as normal completion.

```python
import contextlib


@contextlib.contextmanager
def transaction(conn):
    conn.begin()
    try:
        yield conn
    except Exception:
        conn.rollback()
        raise          # re-raise: see below
    else:
        conn.commit()
```

If the `with` body raises, the decorator does not simply resume the generator - it calls `gen.throw(exc)`, so the exception is raised *at the yield point*, inside your `try`. That is what makes cleanup-on-error possible. The `raise` inside the handler is mandatory: if the handler returns normally, the generator raises `StopIteration` instead, and the decorator interprets that as "the context manager handled it", so the original exception is suppressed and execution continues after the `with` block as if nothing failed. That is the single most common bug in hand-written context managers, and it produces data corruption far more often than a crash would.

The `else` branch runs only when no exception occurred, which is where a commit belongs. A `finally` clause is the right place for unconditional cleanup such as closing a socket or releasing a lock. And because the manager is a generator, it can only be entered once - reusing the same object in two `with` statements raises `RuntimeError: generator didn't yield`, so keep the factory-call form `with transaction(conn):` rather than storing it in a variable.

Rust has three test locations and they serve different purposes, which is why the convention is to use all three rather than picking one.

```text
src/foo.rs      #[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }   can see private items
src/lib.rs      unit tests for internal helpers, invariants, edge cases
tests/api.rs    integration tests: a separate crate, public API only
tests/common/   shared fixtures, MUST NOT be named mod.rs to avoid
                being compiled as its own test binary
/// ```            doc tests: compile and run examples from documentation
```

The distinction that matters is what each can reach. A unit test inside the module can call private functions, which is how you test a parser's internal state machine without exposing it. An integration test in `tests/` links against the crate as an external consumer would, so it only sees the public API - which makes it the check that the public surface is actually usable and complete. If an integration test cannot be written without adding a `pub` that should not exist, that is a real design signal rather than a testing inconvenience.

Doc tests are the most underrated of the three because they are documentation that cannot rot: `cargo test` compiles and runs every example in a doc comment, so an example that stops working fails the build. They are also shown on docs.rs, which means the first thing a user reads is verified code.

```rust
/// Parses a duration string such as `"1h30m"`.
///
/// ```
/// # use mylib::parse_duration;
/// assert_eq!(parse_duration("90m").unwrap().as_secs(), 5400);
/// assert!(parse_duration("90").is_err());
/// ```
///
/// # Errors
/// Returns [`ParseError::MissingUnit`] when a number has no unit suffix.
pub fn parse_duration(input: &str) -> Result<Duration, ParseError> { /* ... */ }
```

Three practical notes. Use `# ` at the start of a doc-test line to hide setup that the reader does not need - the hidden lines are compiled and run but not displayed. Mark a doc test `no_run` when it must compile but cannot execute in CI (network access, a required file), and `ignore` only as a last resort, since an ignored test is documentation that is allowed to be wrong. And keep fixtures for integration tests in `tests/common/mod.rs`: a file directly in `tests/` is compiled as its own test binary, so a shared helper placed there produces a confusing "no tests in this binary" warning.
